\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Diagnostics on the DSCR donor swap (42{} recipients), run on synthetic predictors with known behaviour over 50{} simulations: the donor-tracking gain, the image-vs-text answer-change rate, and the share of simulations in which the permutation flags tracking ($p<.05$). A generic image shift that ignores which donor is shown reaches a positive gain and a high answer-change rate yet is never flagged, and at $\rho =0.4$ a genuine tracker and the generic shift share the same gain (+19.0{} vs +19.0{}~pp): neither the gain nor the answer-change rate separates them, the permutation does.}}{14}{table.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:donorval}{{6}{14}{Diagnostics on the DSCR donor swap (\vfourDonorValN {} recipients), run on synthetic predictors with known behaviour over \vfourDonorValSims {} simulations: the donor-tracking gain, the image-vs-text answer-change rate, and the share of simulations in which the permutation flags tracking ($p<.05$). A generic image shift that ignores which donor is shown reaches a positive gain and a high answer-change rate yet is never flagged, and at $\rho =0.4$ a genuine tracker and the generic shift share the same gain (\vfourDonorValTrackLowGain {} vs \vfourDonorValShiftGain {}~pp): neither the gain nor the answer-change rate separates them, the permutation does}{table.caption.47}{}}
